To upgrade an existing WordPress website with modern, interactive animations, you primarily need JavaScript and CSS. You do not need to rebuild your site in React.

Here is a breakdown of how modern web animation works, what languages are involved, and the best tools to use for a WordPress site, explained without the technical jargon.



The Languages: 

Think of your website like a theater production. To add special effects, you need three different crew members:

* HTML (The Stage & Actors): This is the basic structure of your site. It is the text, the images, and the buttons sitting perfectly still on the page.

* CSS (The Costumes & Lighting): CSS controls how things look (colors, fonts, sizing). It can also handle very simple, everyday movements, like a button changing color when you hover your mouse over it, or a picture gently fading in when the page loads.

* JavaScript (The Director & Special Effects): This is the programming language that makes the real magic happen. Whenever you see complex, modern animations—like images moving at different speeds as you scroll down the page (parallax), graphics following your cursor, or objects spinning and bouncing based on how fast you scroll—JavaScript is the engine doing the heavy lifting.

* React (The Overkill): React is a completely different way to build a website from the ground up, often used for complex apps like Netflix or Facebook. Trying to use React just to add animations to a standard WordPress site is like tearing down your house to install a new lightbulb. Stick to JavaScript and CSS.


How It Is Actually Done Today
Modern web developers rarely write complex JavaScript animations entirely from scratch because it takes too long and can make the website run slowly. Instead, they use pre-built "engines" and WordPress plugins.

1. The Engine: GSAP (GreenSock)
Behind almost every highly animated, award-winning website today is a JavaScript tool called GSAP (GreenSock Animation Platform, https://github.com/greensock/GSAP). It is the industry's gold standard for web animation. It tells elements exactly how and when to move without slowing down the user's browser.   
WordPress.org


2. Visual Animation Plugins (No-Code Tools)
Because raw GSAP requires a developer to write code, the modern WordPress ecosystem has created visual "point-and-click" tools. These plugins put the power of the GSAP engine into a user-friendly interface, allowing you or your developer to animate the site visually:

Motion.page: A popular tool that lets you click on any element on your live site and create complex scroll animations using a visual timeline, very similar to how you would edit a video.

Page Builder Addons (like MotionKit or HappyAddons): If your WordPress site was built using a popular visual editor like Elementor or Gutenberg, there are specific plugins that inject these advanced, scroll-triggered animations right into the editor you already use.   
HappyAddons

To modernize your site, your best path forward is to keep it in WordPress and use a GSAP-powered animation plugin. This allows you to add high-end, scroll-driven JavaScript animations directly to your existing pages quickly, without needing to hire a developer to write complex code from scratch.

